Chapter: TlsServe
  cites Encode chapter TlsEndpoint
  cites Net chapter NetIO
  cites Net chapter TcpTransport
  cites Net chapter NetworkStack
  cites Foreword chapter Tls
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 A TLS 1.3 server on a real socket, so that something other than our own
 client can be the thing that talks to it.

 Every TLS test in this tree is a loopback: our client against our server,
 shuttling records between two endpoints in one process. That proves the
 two halves agree with each other and nothing else. This binary exists to
 be driven by openssl, which agrees with the RFC instead.

 It listens on guest port 9443 for one connection at a time, the pattern
 codex-vm port forwarding expects: boot with `-portfwd <host>:9443` and
 the VM injects a SYN toward this listener for every host client that
 connects.

 THE RECORD BOUNDARY IS THE WHOLE OF THE TRANSPORT WORK. tls-ep-recv
 takes exactly one TLS record. TCP does not deliver records, it delivers
 whatever bytes happened to arrive, so a segment may carry half a record
 or three of them. The loop below accumulates into a buffer and takes
 complete records off the front, which is the one thing the loopback
 tests never had to do because they handed over whole records by
 construction.

 The certificate is the same Ed25519 leaf the loopback tests use, minted
 outside Codex. The harness hands openssl the matching CA, read out of
 the test fixture rather than copied, so there is one source of truth for
 those bytes.

